1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,684
Greetings, fellow aliens!

2
00:00:02,126 --> 00:00:04,425
This is the eleventh episode of Earthlings 101.

3
00:00:04,917 --> 00:00:10,438
Today we will learn about something that is so central to earthlings' life that they are barely ever aware of it: Rituals.

4
00:00:43,758 --> 00:00:51,818
When you observe groups of earthlings, you sometimes see behaviours that obviously serve the purpose of feeding, reproducing, and not dying.

5
00:00:52,288 --> 00:01:00,212
But often you have no idea what in the universe is going on: They do things which have absolutely no practical sense, and make sounds that carry no useful information.

6
00:01:00,573 --> 00:01:03,753
When this happens, odds are that you are actually observing a ritual.

7
00:01:06,032 --> 00:01:09,972
By the way, there are as many definitions of "rituals" as there are anthropologists.

8
00:01:10,572 --> 00:01:15,052
What I describe here is what earthling anthropologists call, "interaction rituals".

9
00:01:16,092 --> 00:01:17,976
Rituals are a bit like common tasks.

10
00:01:18,369 --> 00:01:26,809
When a group of earthlings has a common task to accomplish, they concentrate all together onto this task, share their mood and coordinate their actions, until the task is done.

11
00:01:27,189 --> 00:01:30,683
As a side effect, they earn satisfaction and an increased group spirit.

12
00:01:31,188 --> 00:01:35,828
Now, a ritual is actually the same thing as a common task - only without the common task.

13
00:01:36,548 --> 00:01:40,076
To understand rituals, we have first to understand emotions.

14
00:01:40,479 --> 00:01:41,725
What are emotions?

15
00:01:41,725 --> 00:01:49,525
An emotion is a positive or negative mental state linked to a thing, creature, action, event or idea - like a virtual happyfield or unhappyfield.

16
00:01:50,472 --> 00:01:54,695
The four most important emotions are joy, fear, sadness and anger.

17
00:01:55,020 --> 00:01:57,366
I'll talk more about emotions in another episode.

18
00:01:57,626 --> 00:02:04,301
For now, the important thing to know is that earthlings have an emotion-driven intelligence: They try to move towards happyfields and away from unhappyfields.

19
00:02:06,185 --> 00:02:09,358
But earthlings can't share their emotions and intentions telepathically.

20
00:02:10,034 --> 00:02:16,794
So, the question is: If every earthling is driven by his own emotions, how do earthlings manage to accomplish common tasks?

21
00:02:17,254 --> 00:02:22,834
How do they coordinate actions, and more importantly: How do they coordinate the emotions that drive those actions?

22
00:02:23,374 --> 00:02:28,694
The answer is what might be the single most important social mechanism on earth: Rituals.

23
00:02:28,694 --> 00:02:32,854
In general earthlings are not aware that they perform rituals on a daily basis.

24
00:02:33,214 --> 00:02:38,921
Most of them will associate the word "ritual" with a group of hooded men, performing some secret ceremony in a poorly lit room.

25
00:02:39,765 --> 00:02:43,132
But actually, rituals are omnipresent in everyday life.

26
00:02:43,555 --> 00:02:44,976
Earthlings greeting each other?

27
00:02:45,082 --> 00:02:45,922
A ritual.

28
00:02:46,282 --> 00:02:48,445
A social gathering, a group of musicians?

29
00:02:49,173 --> 00:02:49,774
Rituals.

30
00:02:50,181 --> 00:02:52,401
Earthlings eating together or playing a game?

31
00:02:52,761 --> 00:02:53,687
Also rituals.

32
00:02:54,210 --> 00:02:55,304
A political gathering?

33
00:02:55,682 --> 00:02:57,106
A sports event?

34
00:02:57,106 --> 00:02:58,426
All rituals.

35
00:02:58,786 --> 00:03:01,948
Some rituals are religious, but most are quite profane.

36
00:03:02,394 --> 00:03:03,574
A chat between friends?

37
00:03:04,174 --> 00:03:05,754
Love-birds making sweet love?

38
00:03:06,194 --> 00:03:07,329
Again, rituals.

39
00:03:07,917 --> 00:03:09,291
Earthlings attending a funeral?

40
00:03:09,634 --> 00:03:10,381
A ritual.

41
00:03:10,713 --> 00:03:21,513
But even social activities that serve an actual purpose are kind of rituals: Negotiations, transactions, and any kind of cooperative work, from daily working routine to complex and risky work projects.

42
00:03:23,193 --> 00:03:26,338
Actually, it's hard to find a social activity that is not a ritual!

43
00:03:26,788 --> 00:03:29,068
But let's go back to the hooded men in their crypt.

44
00:03:29,448 --> 00:03:36,568
This scene is good for explaining what happens in a ritual - not because it's a frequent event, but because earthlings think it is exemplary for rituals.

45
00:03:36,568 --> 00:03:39,168
So, what constitutes a ritual?

46
00:03:39,608 --> 00:03:45,428
To perform a ritual, we need earthlings gathering together, and separating themselves from the exterior world.

47
00:03:45,788 --> 00:03:52,800
The ritual usually follows predefined formalities - more or less strict rules what outfit to wear, what objects to use etc.

48
00:03:52,800 --> 00:03:57,340
The earthlings concentrate on the same thing and perform some predefined actions.

49
00:03:57,640 --> 00:04:01,782
The thing they concentrate upon becomes the center of the ritual, a "sacred object".

50
00:04:02,302 --> 00:04:07,329
It can be an actual object, but also a person, an idea, or whatever else can be a "thing".

51
00:04:07,712 --> 00:04:14,928
The ritual actions are usually coordinated, often rhythmic activities, like processions, chants, formulae or body movements.

52
00:04:14,928 --> 00:04:21,048
What matters is actually not the actions per se, but the coordination, the common focus onto the sacred object.

53
00:04:21,448 --> 00:04:28,168
This ritual activity helps establishing a shared emotion: Devotion, joy, pain, anger - that depends on the ritual.

54
00:04:28,688 --> 00:04:35,568
When the ritual is successfully performed, several things happen: First, the members feeling of belonging to this group is strengthened.

55
00:04:36,028 --> 00:04:39,739
In particular, the feeling for what helps or hurts the group is reinforced.

56
00:04:39,739 --> 00:04:44,339
In other words, the group establishes its sense of good and bad, its morality.

57
00:04:44,959 --> 00:04:51,171
Secondly the participants gain "emotional energy" - in other words, the ritual establishes a virtual happyfield of its own.

58
00:04:51,712 --> 00:04:54,499
But you may ask, what happens when the ritual is over?

59
00:04:54,866 --> 00:04:58,265
The happyfield will collapse, the group spirit will vanish, and then?

60
00:04:58,667 --> 00:05:03,159
Wouldn't it be great to have some keepsake of the rituals, something the members can take home?

61
00:05:03,741 --> 00:05:06,049
That's where the third effect comes in: Symbols.

62
00:05:06,049 --> 00:05:10,016
A symbol is some kind of portable happyfield capacitor to conserve the ritual happyfield.

63
00:05:11,008 --> 00:05:16,045
It can be an actual object, a sign, an idea, a person, a word or anything else.

64
00:05:16,551 --> 00:05:19,920
Often, the symbol is actually the sacred object the ritual was about.

65
00:05:20,364 --> 00:05:26,138
It's a bit like a souvenir - those little trinkets earthlings bring back home from a voyage as a keepsake of good moments.

66
00:05:26,400 --> 00:05:33,740
A symbol doesn't only make happy, but also serves as a reminder for shared emotions, group spirit and group morality - till the next ritual.

67
00:05:33,740 --> 00:05:35,920
Because there is always a next ritual.

68
00:05:36,400 --> 00:05:40,328
Rituals are repeated more or less frequently - this is called ritual chains.

69
00:05:40,692 --> 00:05:48,152
Each group has its own ritual chains where the group reinforces its coherency, establishes its moral values and creates and charges its symbols.

70
00:05:48,712 --> 00:06:00,752
All rituals consist of the same components we just identified: - a group of earthlings gathering together, - Predefined formal rules, - concentration on a sacred object, - shared emotions, - and coordinated, often rhythmic actions.

71
00:06:00,752 --> 00:06:12,132
On the outcome side, we have - positive emotions, like happiness or solace - a reinforced group spirit, - establishment of moral group standards, - and the charging of group spirit capacitors - symbols.

72
00:06:12,852 --> 00:06:18,394
Modern earthlings often display "their" symbols on t-shirts, casual clothing they wear on the upper body.

73
00:06:18,854 --> 00:06:23,345
"Casual" means that the clothing is not required by formal dress-codes of other ritual chains.

74
00:06:23,345 --> 00:06:28,145
As a rule of thumb, when you can put something onto a t-shirt, it's probably a symbol.

75
00:06:28,705 --> 00:06:35,325
By the way, in case my earthlings viewers want t-shirts with symbols from my show, I've created an online t-shirt shop.

76
00:06:35,725 --> 00:06:36,865
You'll find the link in the doobly-doo.

77
00:06:38,025 --> 00:06:39,644
Symbols don't need to be graphical.

78
00:06:40,184 --> 00:06:52,590
Intimate sexual relationships, for example, are built and maintained by ritual chains called "romance" which create all kinds of symbols: Words, objects, plants, even sounds - often called, "our song".

79
00:06:52,590 --> 00:06:56,870
Acoustical symbols are also used by large groups, like nations.

80
00:06:57,590 --> 00:07:00,024
National musical symbols are called "anthems".

81
00:07:00,354 --> 00:07:08,914
Other national symbols include famous earthlings, food, drinks, clothes, historical events, words, colors, shapes and creatures.

82
00:07:09,514 --> 00:07:12,261
Actually, almost everything can become a national symbol.

83
00:07:12,644 --> 00:07:14,684
Except for the microbes.

84
00:07:18,064 --> 00:07:19,470
Tips for tourists.

85
00:07:19,470 --> 00:07:22,710
A concert is a strange but entertaining type of ritual.

86
00:07:23,010 --> 00:07:27,251
If you do what the other spectators do, you may stay unnoticed in the crowd.

87
00:07:27,834 --> 00:07:30,914
However, don't imitate the musicians, you might get into trouble.

88
00:07:31,434 --> 00:07:34,175
Let's look at a common ritual: A public ball game.

89
00:07:34,675 --> 00:07:37,970
Usually, games are performed in series - ritual chains.

90
00:07:38,516 --> 00:07:40,962
Here we have player teams and fans gathered together.

91
00:07:40,962 --> 00:07:45,827
The players perform a highly ritualized game which follows strict rules and requires a lot of coordination.

92
00:07:46,330 --> 00:07:53,753
But the fans do also coordinated, rhythmic actions, like chanting or choreographic wave movements, to express their shared emotions.

93
00:07:54,115 --> 00:07:58,791
For the players, the sacred object is the ball, for the fans it's rather "their" team.

94
00:07:58,791 --> 00:08:07,557
On the outcome side, we have an emotional happyfield, a reinforced group spirit and often shared feelings of morality, for example against referees perceived as unfair.

95
00:08:08,000 --> 00:08:15,620
And to maintain these achievements after the ritual, there are the symbols: Team emblems, color codes, and sometimes a trophy for the winning team.

96
00:08:17,520 --> 00:08:18,556
Scientific advice.

97
00:08:18,937 --> 00:08:22,935
The advantage of open air rituals is that they are easy to observe from high altitude.

98
00:08:23,521 --> 00:08:29,201
If you want to learn what happens when the ritual is disturbed, just beam a cow onto the playing field and observe the reaction.

99
00:08:31,641 --> 00:08:34,043
Perhaps the most common rituals are conversations.

100
00:08:34,495 --> 00:08:39,187
A conversation often starts with a greeting - a ritual we already encountered in episode 7.

101
00:08:39,547 --> 00:08:44,214
Greetings usually involve touching each other in a synchronized, even choreographic manner.

102
00:08:44,214 --> 00:08:49,254
The actual conversation may start with the question "How are you", in order to synchronize emotions.

103
00:08:49,634 --> 00:09:00,194
What follows is a turn-taking exchange of sentences that may or may not carry actual information - sometimes earthlings just speak about atmospheric conditions or events everybody has already heard of.

104
00:09:00,514 --> 00:09:02,533
Earthlings call this "small talk".

105
00:09:02,686 --> 00:09:08,922
The goal of small talk is not information sharing, but sharing of emotions and the activity of turn-taking speaking itself.

106
00:09:08,922 --> 00:09:15,767
In Great Britain and America, gaps or overlaps between turns in a friendly conversation are typically shorter than a tenth of a second.

107
00:09:16,170 --> 00:09:19,870
Longer gaps are perceived as "awkward", longer overlaps as "unfriendly".

108
00:09:21,530 --> 00:09:25,461
Sometimes, conversation rituals lead to judging outsiders as immoral or awkward.

109
00:09:25,836 --> 00:09:27,785
This kind of conversation is called "gossip".

110
00:09:28,237 --> 00:09:31,575
This is an example for rituals strengthening a sense of morality.

111
00:09:31,575 --> 00:09:37,932
In larger groups, this can grow to a public outrage against other groups, which can lead to riots and pogroms.

112
00:09:38,293 --> 00:09:49,762
A recent example for those hate rituals is that of public protests in France by a conservative minority, against a law that gives homosexuals the right to do something called marriage, formerly a privilege of heterosexuals.

113
00:09:50,043 --> 00:09:53,150
As a general rule, Earthlings don't like to share their privileges.

114
00:09:53,897 --> 00:09:56,935
Marriage is a formal act to officialise a sexual relationship.

115
00:09:56,935 --> 00:09:59,306
But why is this formality so important?

116
00:09:59,715 --> 00:10:01,763
The answer is simple: It's a ritual!

117
00:10:05,007 --> 00:10:11,347
The marriage ritual strengthens not only the bond of the couple itself, but also admits the couple to the group of married citizens.

118
00:10:11,767 --> 00:10:14,847
And many members of this group don't want homosexuals in their club.

119
00:10:15,747 --> 00:10:22,675
An important kind of rituals is punishments, unpleasant actions imposed upon individuals that have violated the moral standards of the group.

120
00:10:22,675 --> 00:10:28,355
That can be a monetary penalty, imprisonment, torture, public humiliation, exile or death.

121
00:10:28,555 --> 00:10:34,127
The goal is not only to discourage violators, but also to ritually restitute the damaged group integrity.

122
00:10:34,735 --> 00:10:36,123
Earthlings call this "justice".

123
00:10:36,752 --> 00:10:46,752
Another important type is rituals of passage, which mark earthlings' transition to a new period of life: Being born, entering adulthood, graduating from school, marriage, death.

124
00:10:48,052 --> 00:10:53,372
Entering a social group can also be made by a rituals of passage - those are called initiation rituals.

125
00:10:54,192 --> 00:11:01,252
Rites of passage often include physical pain, fear or another kind of suffering - from simple practical jokes up to life-threatening ordeals.

126
00:11:02,172 --> 00:11:10,962
That's also the reason why final exams at schools or universities are usually very stressful - even if the candidate has already proven their ability by writing a scientific paper.

127
00:11:11,364 --> 00:11:13,944
Exams are rites of passage, they have to hurt!

128
00:11:16,424 --> 00:11:17,544
Strategic Advice.

129
00:11:18,164 --> 00:11:22,010
Conquering earth isn't really difficult, given your technological superiority.

130
00:11:22,631 --> 00:11:25,211
But ruling the planet afterwards is a wholly different thing.

131
00:11:25,891 --> 00:11:29,237
To stay in power, you will need mass rituals and strong symbols.

132
00:11:29,870 --> 00:11:34,576
However, choose your symbols carefully: Offensive or funny symbols may ruin your authority.

133
00:11:34,919 --> 00:11:39,973
This episode is mainly based upon the book "Interaction ritual chains" by Randall Collins.

134
00:11:39,973 --> 00:11:43,433
According to the book, rituals require bodily presence.

135
00:11:43,873 --> 00:11:45,733
However, I'm not sure about that.

136
00:11:45,993 --> 00:11:55,493
In the recent years, one could observe the rise of strong online communities which create their own group identity, group symbols and a common sense of morality, even if they never meet in person.

137
00:11:55,753 --> 00:12:01,645
This might indicate the existence of some online equivalent to interaction rituals, which hold those communities together.

138
00:12:01,645 --> 00:12:06,105
So, Earthlings use rituals for pretty much every social activity.

139
00:12:06,405 --> 00:12:07,165
But why?

140
00:12:07,505 --> 00:12:08,985
What's the purpose of rituals?

141
00:12:09,505 --> 00:12:12,265
I think rituals are actually a substitute for telepathy.

142
00:12:12,975 --> 00:12:30,015
See, like most social civilizations, earthlings have different perceived planes of reality: The physical world, the plane of emotions, and social reality, which includes things like social status, relationships, groups, gratitude, karma etc. - all those mechanisms which make up society.

143
00:12:30,516 --> 00:12:33,036
Actually, this is the plane where society happens.

144
00:12:33,596 --> 00:12:38,800
There is a fourth plane, the legal reality - a formalization of some aspects of social reality.

145
00:12:39,221 --> 00:12:41,021
I'll speak about this in another episode.

146
00:12:41,861 --> 00:12:45,519
Normally, a civilization would manage those planes via telepathy.

147
00:12:45,519 --> 00:12:57,239
The Yetlings of Arcturus 11, for example, are driven by some kind of emotions, form groups and personal relationships, and have a virtual exchange currency, not unlike gratitude, but shared within a group.

148
00:12:57,659 --> 00:13:02,195
Now, being emotion-driven beings, Yetlings need to share emotions telepathically.

149
00:13:02,617 --> 00:13:05,437
Also, they use telepathy to act on the social plane.

150
00:13:06,317 --> 00:13:08,548
Mechanisms like this are common in the galaxy.

151
00:13:08,548 --> 00:13:18,409
Some alien scientists have even theorized that a society isn't possible without telepathy, because only telepathy allows the establishment and continuous synchronization of a common dynamic social reality.

152
00:13:18,729 --> 00:13:24,669
The common telepathic consciousness provides, so they say, the medium where the social reality happens.

153
00:13:24,969 --> 00:13:31,669
This sounds quite convincing, in theory - but then there is this big, annoying, paradoxical example: Earthlings.

154
00:13:31,949 --> 00:13:37,405
Highly social beings, which built up a complex society without any telepathy - how is this possible?

155
00:13:37,405 --> 00:13:40,125
The answer is simple: Rituals.

156
00:13:40,625 --> 00:13:45,202
Symbols and rituals provide the connection between reality and other perceived planes of existence.

157
00:13:45,585 --> 00:13:48,925
When earthlings want to synchronize emotions, they use rituals.

158
00:13:49,485 --> 00:13:55,385
When they want to act upon groups, status, gratitude, relationships etc., they do it through rituals.

159
00:13:56,005 --> 00:13:58,293
Even actions on the legal level require rituals.

160
00:13:58,880 --> 00:14:01,637
Earthling society as we know it wouldn't be possible without rituals.

161
00:14:02,388 --> 00:14:08,268
Of course, earthlings don't do rituals because they are important for society - they do them because they enjoy them.

162
00:14:08,508 --> 00:14:14,654
And whenever earthlings enjoy something, you can be pretty sure that it's again a scheme of our old friend, the genetic imperative.

163
00:14:15,277 --> 00:14:16,297
What a surprise.

164
00:14:16,997 --> 00:14:19,417
A note concerning my closed AdSense account.

165
00:14:19,905 --> 00:14:23,494
First of all, thank you all for your encouraging messages and ideas.

166
00:14:23,494 --> 00:14:29,474
However, I decided not to look for alternative ways to monetize my videos, except the t-shirt shop.

167
00:14:29,934 --> 00:14:33,575
Anyway, I don't do this for a living - this is what earthlings call a hobby.

168
00:14:33,955 --> 00:14:36,472
This was the eleventh episode of Earthlings 101.

169
00:14:37,053 --> 00:14:41,217
In the next episode we will learn about the earthlings' most important sense: Vision.

170
00:14:41,641 --> 00:14:46,240
We will also learn why earthlings call this "yellow", although it's actually a pattern of red and green.

171
00:14:47,131 --> 00:14:52,610
Thanks for watching, click on "like" if you enjoyed the video, and as always: Don't forget to be alien!

